{"name":"Cooled Blue","hex":"#75b9ae","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cooled","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Brought to a lower temperature.","With a descriptive word: brought to a lower temperature by means of."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of cool"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cooled"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cooling","blocks":[{"text":"Cooling is removal of heat, usually resulting in a lower temperature and/or phase change. Temperature lowering achieved by any other means may also be called cooling.\nThe transfer of thermal energy may occur via thermal radiation, heat conduction or convection. Examples can be as simple as reducing temperature of tea."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Cooling_fan_on_Harman_Kardon_AVR_370_AV_receiver.jpg/330px-Cooling_fan_on_Harman_Kardon_AVR_370_AV_receiver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cooling","wikidata":"Q3929317"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Evaporative_cooler_in_front_of_Hakata_Station.jpg/500px-Evaporative_cooler_in_front_of_Hakata_Station.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Evaporative_cooler_in_front_of_Hakata_Station.jpg/1280px-Evaporative_cooler_in_front_of_Hakata_Station.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Evaporative_cooler_in_front_of_Hakata_Station.jpg/500px-Evaporative_cooler_in_front_of_Hakata_Station.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An evaporative cooler near Hakata Station in Fukuoka, Japan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEvaporative_cooler_in_front_of_Hakata_Station.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Pumpable_ice_maker_and_storage_tank_installed_in_a_basement_of_Cyprus_Olimpic_supermarket.JPG/500px-Pumpable_ice_maker_and_storage_tank_installed_in_a_basement_of_Cyprus_Olimpic_supermarket.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Pumpable_ice_maker_and_storage_tank_installed_in_a_basement_of_Cyprus_Olimpic_supermarket.JPG/1280px-Pumpable_ice_maker_and_storage_tank_installed_in_a_basement_of_Cyprus_Olimpic_supermarket.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_conte